facts:
$y=a^x$ with $a>1$ is **increasing**, which is exponential **growth**.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-3.4,0) -- (3.4,0);
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (0,-1.6) -- (0,3.3);
\node at (3.65,-0.3) {$x$};
\node at (-0.35,3.5) {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black,<->,domain=-2.7:1.6,smooth,samples=80,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{exp(\x*ln(2))});
\node[font=\small] at (-1.85,-0.8) {horizontal asymptote};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-2.2,-0.55) -- (-2.5,-0.07);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$y=a^x$ with $0<a<1$ is **decreasing**, which is exponential **decay**. For $y=a^{-x}$, the curve is decreasing when $a>1$ and increasing when $0<a<1$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-3.4,0) -- (3.4,0);
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (0,-1.6) -- (0,3.3);
\node at (3.65,-0.3) {$x$};
\node at (-0.35,3.5) {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue!70!black,<->,domain=-1.6:2.7,smooth,samples=80,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{exp(-\x*ln(2))});
\node[font=\small] at (1.85,-0.8) {horizontal asymptote};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (2.2,-0.55) -- (2.5,-0.07);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
The base $6>1$, so the curve is increasing: exponential growth.

For $x=0$:
$\begin{align*}y&=6^0\\y&=1\end{align*}$

The $y$-intercept is $(0,1)$. The horizontal asymptote is $y=0$.

As $x\to\infty$, $y\to\infty$. As $x\to-\infty$, $y\to0$ from above.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,<->] (-2.5,0)--(1.5,0);
\draw[black,<->] (0,-0.6)--(0,6.5);
\path (1.5,0) node[below] {$x$} (0,6.5) node[left] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-2.3:1,samples=100,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{exp(\x*ln(6))});
\fill[black] (0,1) circle (1.5pt);
\path (0,1) node[above left] {$(0,1)$} (-1.4,0) node[below=5mm] {$y=0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Using index laws:
$\begin{align*}7^{-x}&=(7^{-1})^x\\&=\left(\frac{1}{7}\right)^x\end{align*}$

The equations describe the same curve. Its base $\frac{1}{7}$ is between $0$ and $1$, so it is decreasing: exponential decay.

For $x=0$:
$\begin{align*}y&=7^0\\y&=1\end{align*}$

The $y$-intercept is $(0,1)$ and the horizontal asymptote is $y=0$.

As $x\to\infty$, $y\to0$ from above. As $x\to-\infty$, $y\to\infty$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,<->] (-1.5,0)--(2.5,0);
\draw[black,<->] (0,-0.6)--(0,7.5);
\path (2.5,0) node[below] {$x$} (0,7.5) node[left] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-1:2.3,samples=100,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{exp(-\x*ln(7))});
\fill[black] (0,1) circle (1.5pt);
\path (0,1) node[above right] {$(0,1)$} (1.5,0) node[below=5mm] {$y=0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
The graph of $y=-4^x+2$ is shown.

(a) Read the horizontal asymptote from the graph.

(b) Calculate the $y$-intercept.

(c) Describe what happens as $x\to\infty$ and as $x\to-\infty$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=12mm,y=9mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[gridgrey,xstep=12mm,ystep=9mm] (-3,-3) grid (1,3);
\draw[black,<->] (-3.3,0)--(1.5,0);
\draw[black,<->] (0,-3.3)--(0,3.4);
\path (1.5,0) node[below] {$x$} (0,3.4) node[left] {$y$};
\foreach \v in {-3,-2,-1,1} {\draw[black] (\v,0.06)--(\v,-0.06);\path (\v,-0.06) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\foreach \v in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} {\draw[black] (0.06,\v)--(-0.06,\v);\path (-0.06,\v) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-3:1.15,samples=100,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2-exp(\x*ln(4))});
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
(a) The left end of the curve approaches the horizontal line $y=2$ from below. The asymptote is $y=2$.

(b) For $x=0$:
$\begin{align*}y&=-4^0+2\\y&=-1+2\\y&=1\end{align*}$

The $y$-intercept is $(0,1)$.

(c) As $x\to\infty$, $4^x\to\infty$, so $y\to-\infty$.

As $x\to-\infty$, $4^x\to0$, so $y\to2$ from below.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=12mm,y=9mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{asymptotered}{HTML}{EF6068}
